# Blocks branching on the exit code of a pipe that ends in head/tail/wc.
#
# NOT once-per-session: like the jest rules, this is a hard stop on a mechanical
# mistake rather than a nudge toward a skill.
#
# Why: head, tail and wc exit 0 whatever they were fed — including nothing at all.
# So `cmd | wc -l || echo none` reports success when `cmd` failed AND when it
# printed nothing, and the two are indistinguishable. The repo has been bitten by
# exactly this: `|| echo "none"` printed "none" over a command that never ran,
# and a `grep -c ... | head` reported three skills as lacking coverage they had.
#
# DELIBERATELY NARROW. `cmd | grep -q x && ...` is idiomatic and correct — grep's
# exit code IS the answer there — so grep is not matched. Only head/tail/wc are,
# because branching on them is wrong every time, with no legitimate case to weigh.
# A rule that fires on good code gets disabled, and then it protects nothing.
#
# ...WITH ONE EXCEPTION, ADDED 2026-09-01: `grep -c`.
#
# The exemption above was too broad by exactly one flag, and the reasoning shows
# why. With `-q`, grep's exit code IS the answer. With `-c` it is NOT: grep prints
# a count AND exits 1 when that count is zero. So `grep -c ... || echo 0` fires the
# fallback on a perfectly good "0", and the substitution captures TWO lines:
#
#     B=$(git show "$rev:$f" | grep -cE 'pattern' || echo 0)   # -> "0\n0"
#
# Every later `[ "$A" -lt "$B" ]` then died with "integer expression expected"
# while the summary printed a clean zero — a verification that checked 80 files,
# errored on every one, and reported all-clear. The script was written to confirm
# no test had been weakened, so a false pass there is the worst possible place for
# one.
#
# `-c` only. `-q`, `-l`, `-E` and the rest stay exempt, because their exit codes
# mean what a branch expects them to mean.
#
# The fix is always the same shape: capture into a variable, then test that.
#   n=$(cmd | wc -l | tr -d ' '); [ "$n" -gt 0 ] && ...
#
# Rule lives in the architecture handbook, section "Checking things".
rule_id=piped-exit-code
rule_once=0

rule_match() {
    [ "$1" = "Bash" ] || return 1
    # A pipe into head/tail/wc, then || or && consuming its status.
    # [^|] also matches NEWLINE, so the span could reach a branch belonging to a
    # LATER command in a multi-line block and fire on it. Found 2026-08-30 when this
    # rule blocked a verification script whose pipe and branch were four lines apart
    # and unrelated — and then blocked the command that came to fix it. Excluding
    # newline keeps the match inside one command.
    # The span excludes COMMAND SEPARATORS, not a newline. It read [^|\n] until
    # 2026-09-01, which on BSD grep is a bracket expression holding the literal
    # characters backslash and "n" -- so it silently stopped at the first letter
    # "n" in any argument. Control: grep -oE 'a[^|\n]*' on abcnxyz|end returns
    # abc. Latent since 2026-08-30; surfaced when the grep arm below missed the
    # word "pattern" and nothing else. Excluding |;& is what the original comment
    # wanted -- keep the match inside ONE command -- and newlines never needed
    # excluding, because the router turns them into spaces before any rule runs.
    if printf '%s' "$2" | grep -qE '\|[[:space:]]*(head|tail|wc)([ \t][^|;&]*)?[[:space:]]*(\|\||&&)'
    then
        return 0
    fi
    # `grep -c` branched on. Requires the -c flag, so -q/-l/-E stay exempt; `-c`
    # may sit inside a combined cluster (-cE), hence the character class either
    # side. `--count` is spelled out because the cluster branch cannot reach it.
    printf '%s' "$2" | grep -qE \
        'grep[^|;&]*([[:space:]]-[a-zA-Z]*c[a-zA-Z]*|--count)([ \t][^|;&]*)?[[:space:]]*(\|\||&&)'
}

rule_message() {
    cat <<'MSG'
Blocked: head, tail and wc exit 0 whatever they were fed — including nothing.

So `|| echo none` and `&& ...` after one of them report the PIPE's success, not
the command's. A failure and an empty result look identical, and both look like
a pass.

Capture the value, then test it:
  n=$(cmd | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
  [ "$n" -gt 0 ] || echo "really none"

`grep -c` is blocked for a DIFFERENT reason, and it is the sneakier one:

  n=$(cmd | grep -cE 'pattern' || echo 0)     ->  captures "0\n0"

grep -c prints a count AND exits 1 when the count is zero, so the fallback fires
on a perfectly good "0" and you get two lines. Every later `[ "$n" -gt 0 ]` then
dies with "integer expression expected" while your summary still prints a clean
zero. On 2026-09-01 that made a script check 80 files, error on every one, and
report all-clear.

  n=$(cmd | grep -cE 'pattern')      # no fallback needed — it already prints 0

`grep -q` and `grep -l` are NOT blocked — `cmd | grep -q x && ...` is correct,
because there grep's exit code is the answer you actually want.

Doing real counting or comparison? Write it in Python and give it a positive
control. Shell arithmetic over command output is where silent zeros come from.
MSG
}
